{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "03-systems.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Solving systems by row reduction",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Solve a system of linear equations by reducing its augmented matrix, and read each row operation.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The system $x + 2y = 5,\\ 3x + 4y = 6$ is the augmented matrix `[1, 2, 5; 3, 4, 6]`: one row per equation, the right-hand sides in the last column.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Row operations keep the solutions\n> Swapping two rows, scaling a row by a nonzero number, and adding a multiple of one row to another do not change the solution set. Each of the three is proved in Lean (the green dots), and so is the claim that the result is in reduced row echelon form.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, 2, 5; 3, 4, 6])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Read it off: the first row says $x = -4$, the second $y = \\frac92$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!intuition] Row operations turn the lines\n> Each equation in two unknowns is a line, and the solution is where the lines cross. A combination of two equations holds wherever both do, so its line passes through the same crossing. Row reduction turns the lines about that point until they stand level and upright, where the answer can be read off.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 2\nview -7.5, 3.5, -0.8, 6.8\nlet a2 = 3 - 3*t\nlet b2 = 4 - 6*t\nlet c2 = 6 - 15*t\nlet b1 = 4 - 2*t\nlet c1 = 14 - 9*t\nL1 = line([1, 2], [-1, 3]) thick color 3\nL2 = line(c2/(a2^2 + b2^2)*[a2, b2], c2/(a2^2 + b2^2)*[a2, b2] + [-b2, a2]) thick color 4\nL2b = line([0, 4.5], [1, 4.5]) thick color 4\nL1m = line(c1/(1 + b1^2)*[1, b1], c1/(1 + b1^2)*[1, b1] + [-b1, 1]) thick color 3\nX = point([-4, 4.5]) thick color 1\nLX = label([-4, 4.5], \"(-4, \\tfrac92)\") color 1\n> show L1, L2, X, LX | Each equation is a line: $x + 2y = 5$ (green) and $3x + 4y = 6$ (pink). The solution is where they cross.\n> play t to 1 in 3s | Subtract $3$ times the first row from the second. The pink line turns about the crossing, and ends level: $-2y = -9$.\n> hide L2; show L2b | Scale the second row by $-\\frac12$: $y = \\frac92$. The same line, written more simply.\n> hide L1; show L1m; play t to 2 in 3s | Subtract twice the second row from the first. Now the green line turns, about the same point, until it stands upright: $x = -4$.\n> | Reduced, each line names one coordinate, and the crossing never moved: $x = -4$, $y = \\frac92$.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Three equations",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([2, 1, -1, 8; -3, -1, 2, -11; -2, 1, 2, -3])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Check the solution $x = 2,\\ y = 3,\\ z = -1$: put it into the first equation, $2x + y - z$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst(subst(subst(2x + y - z, x, 2), y, 3), z, -1)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, 1, 3; 1, -1, 1])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Solve $x + y = 3,\\ x - y = 1$ by reducing the augmented matrix. Give the reduced matrix.",
      "hints": [
        "The augmented matrix is `[1, 1, 3; 1, -1, 1]`.",
        "Subtract the first row from the second, then scale."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([2, 4, 6; 1, 3, 4])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce the augmented matrix of $2x + 4y = 6,\\ x + 3y = 4$.",
      "hints": [
        "Scale the first row by $\\frac12$ first."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Row reduction is elimination written on the matrix: three operations, none of which changes the solutions, until each variable stands alone in its row.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
